    "body": "## Problem\n\nThe first `win_viewport` event of a new window computes `scroll_delta` with `win_text_height()` from line 1 up to the topline, because `w_viewport_last_botline` is still 0 and the \"scrolled more than a screen\" approximation is skipped. This runs for `curwin` with any UI, including the TUI.\n\nWith `'conceallevel'` >= 2, `win_text_height()` checks each of those lines for `conceal_lines`, which for treesitter highlighting means a parse and queries per line. So opening a large file in a new window (`:tabedit`/`:split`/`:new` with a restore-cursor autocmd, `nvim_open_win()`, `nvim +N file`) and jumping far before the first redraw blocks for seconds. `:edit` in an existing window is not affected.\n\nThis is the \"cursor at the bottom\" case in\nhttps://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/39699#issuecomment-5461843041.\n\n## Solution\n\nInitialize `w_viewport_last_topline`/`w_viewport_last_botline` from the initial `w_topline`/`w_botline`, so the first delta is approximated like any other scroll by more than a screen (as documented for `win_viewport`).\n\nWithout wrapped lines or closed folds above the viewport the reported value is unchanged (`topline - 1`, checked by the multigrid test). Otherwise the first delta of a new window now counts buffer lines instead of screen lines, e.g. a closed fold over lines 2-900 with the cursor at line 1000 gives 988 instead of 90.\n\nNot changed here: `win_split()` copies `w_topline` but not `w_viewport_last_*`, so a split of a scrolled window still reports a scroll from line 1 (now cheap). Fixing that changes what UIs see, so I left it for a separate PR.\n\n## Benchmark\n\nRelease build, TUI 160x50, `nvim --clean`, `conceallevel=2`, Markdown with treesitter highlighting, until `:redraw` returns:\n\n| file | action | before | after |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 5000 lines, mixed (headings, lists, fenced code) | `:tabedit` + `4000G` | 18.6 s | 0.20 s |\n| same | `:edit` + `4000G` (unaffected path) | 0.24 s | 0.21 s |\n| 8000 lines of `N some **bold text**` (#39699) | `nvim -c 'normal! 7600G'` | 117 s | 1.9 s |\n\nThe remaining 1.9 s is the per-line cost #39699 is mainly about; this PR does not touch it and does not overlap with #41913. That per-line cost grew about 13x for injection-heavy Markdown with #41734, which is what made this path take seconds instead of ~1 s.\n",
